# This allows "Disco.events" to be shortened to "events"
# You can get the same effect by adding "Disco" 
# to your state autoload shortcuts
# https://github.com/nathanhoad/godot_dialogue_manager/blob/main/docs/Conditions_Mutations.md#state-shortcuts
using Disco

~ start
Fireye: Welcome to the demo! Play through this thing to get an idea of how disco tools works.
Fireye: Here I'm checking the state of an event, and branching based on the outcome.
if boolEventStatus("excited"):
	Fireye: Here's a test branch! (Excited is success)
else:
	Fireye: Here's a test branch, or whatever. (Excited is not success)
Fireye: Now I'll demo a check. Play around with the check parameters yourself to see how it affects the result
Fireye: Oh look a rock! Should I jump over it?
- Sure ({{rollOddsPlayer("jump_over_rock")}}% Success)
	Fireye: Rolls must be on their own lines for async (You can't $> if(rollPlayer()):)
	$> rollPlayer("jump_over_rock"):
	Fireye: Disco.rollResult is automatically written after a roll
	if rollResult:
		Fireye: Hell yeah! Nice jump!
	else: 
		Fireye: *BONK* OW MY NOSE! THIS WAS A BAD IDEA
- Nah
	Fireye: Booooring

=> save_and_load

~ save_and_load

####### State reset demo #######

Fireye: State is saved to a special path automatically on game start
Fireye: You can reset to this saved state by calling resetState()
Fireye: This will echo the current value of the "trigger" event.
Fireye: Trigger is {{events["trigger"].status}} 
Fireye: You can change it in the state.events section of the Disco autoload

$> events["trigger"].status = EEventStatus.SUCCESS # 1
Fireye: Trigger has changed to {{events["trigger"].status}}

# Resets state to game start
$> resetState() # 0
Fireye: State reset! Trigger is now {{events["trigger"].status}}

####### Standard Save & load demo #######
Fireye: Let's test manual saves
$> events["trigger"].status = EEventStatus.SUCCESS # 1
Fireye: Trigger has changed to {{events["trigger"].status}} and the state has been Saved.

$> save_state()
$> events["trigger"].status = EEventStatus.FAILURE # 2
Fireye: Trigger has been changed to {{events["trigger"].status}} 

$> load_state() # 1
Fireye: Save Loaded! Trigger is now {{events["trigger"].status}}

Fireye: Saves overwrite each other, unless provided another path as an argument
$> events["trigger"].status = EEventStatus.FAILURE # 2
Fireye: Trigger has been changed to {{events["trigger"].status}} and the state has been saved! 
$> save_state()
$> load_state() # 2
Fireye: Save Loaded! Trigger is now {{events["trigger"].status}}, showing the previous save has been overwritten

=> data

~ data
Fireye: Now let's test data!
Fireye: The data system is disco tools' key/value store
Fireye: We have an int called "iterator" set to {{getData("iterator")}}
Fireye: Now give me a name!
- Mike
	# You can also access data values like this:
	$> data["playerName"].value = "Mike"
- Emily
	$> setData("playerName", "Emily")
- Balthazar the Consumer 
	$> setData("playerName", "Balthazar the Consumer")
Fireye: Now let's branch off that iterator
if getData("iterator") > 10:
	Fireye: {{getData("playerName")}} is cool!
else:
	Fireye: {{getData("playerName")}} is fine I guess.
Fireye: Data values leverage the godot Variant type. Use it responsibly!
=> END    
